 author's intentional comedic escalation paired with the 💞 emoji; dropping it loses the article's signature line."}]},{"id":"ja-advisor-B-4","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Now tell me, how do I hold your contraption again? No author intent.","targetQuote":"で、この仕掛けをどう使えばいいんだ？作者の意図がない。","replacement":"で、この仕掛け、どこを持てばいいんだ？作者の意図がない。","rationale":"\"How do I hold\" continues the knife/handle metaphor from the code block; 「どう使えば」 flattens it into generic 'how do I use'. 「どこを持てば」 preserves the physical-comedy image.","alternative":"で、この代物、どうやって持てばいいんだ？","confidence":0.85,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"Japanese software developers","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The surrounding code aliases Knife as Handle; 'hold' is the deliberate callback."}]},{"id":"ja-advisor-B-5","kind":"translation","category":"readability","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Incorrect (any more). Configure/update your tools.","targetQuote":"間違い（もう）。ツールを設定・更新しよう。","replacement":"今はもう間違いだ。ツールを設定・更新しよう。","rationale":"「間違い（もう）」 is parenthetical translationese that reads as broken Japanese. The parenthetical '(any more)' is simply 'no longer true' and belongs in the sentence flow.","alternative":"（今はもう）間違いだ。ツールを設定・更新しよう。","confidence":0.9,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"Japanese software developers","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"'(any more)' is an inline temporal qualifier, not a separate aside worth bracketing in Japanese."}]},{"id":"ja-advisor-B-6","kind":"translation","category":"grammarSpelling","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"“Here’s some related logic. Also, expect class-ish behavior.“","targetQuote":"「関連ロジックはこちら。ついでに、クラスっぽい動作を期待して。「","replacement":"「関連ロジックはこちら。ついでに、クラスっぽい動作を期待して。」","rationale":"The closing bracket is the wrong character (「 instead of 」) — a visible typo in the summary table.","alternative":"","confidence":0.99,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"Japanese software developers","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"Japanese quotation marks must close with 」; the opening 「 is duplicated."}]},{"id":"ja-advisor-B-7","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":1,"sourceQuote":"A `named export` says it’s “definitely **_A THING!_**”","targetQuote":"`named export`は「間違いなく**_一つのものだ！_**」と言う。","replacement":"`named export`は「間違いなく**_ちゃんとした何かだ！_**」と言う。","rationale":"\"A THING\" in English idiom means 'a real, legitimate thing', not merely 'one item'. 「一つのものだ」 reads flatly literal; 「ちゃんとした何か」 conveys the 'this is a real thing' nuance while keeping the caps emphasis.","alternative":"「間違いなく**_立派な一モノだ！_**」","confidence":0.6,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"Japanese software developers; stylistic preference","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The capitalized 'A THING!' is idiomatic emphasis on legitimacy, not count."}]}]
